         Feeding one of those numbers straight into an inline style like
         `left: Npx` sets an AUTHORED value inside that same zoomed
         context, which the browser then zooms AGAIN on render -- e.g. at
         zoom 1.1, a measured 335px bar was coming out styled as
         `width:335px` but rendering at 335*1.1 = 368px, visibly wider
         than the real bar (and mis-positioned to match). Dividing every
         zoomed measurement by the zoom factor before writing it back
         cancels that out -- the browser's own re-zoom lands it back on
         the real value. Only needed for MEASURED numbers, not the GAP_*
         constants above -- those are meant to scale with zoom like any
         other authored px value on the page. */
      var zoomFactor = parseFloat(D.body.style.zoom) || 1;
      var bars = D.querySelectorAll('.product-form-buttons, .product-form__buttons');
      for (var i = 0; i < bars.length; i++) {
